course: everyday
lesson: 124
title: Handshakes, waves and greetings around the world
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: A good handshake is firm but not hard, about two or three shakes: a floppy hand seems unfriendly and a crushing one hurts.
explain: When your hands are dirty or you have a cold, you explain and wave or smile instead, because nobody wants a sticky hand and germs spread through hands.
explain: Some people do not shake hands, for example for religious reasons; you never insist, and a smile, a nod or a hand on your heart is fine.
explain: Greetings differ around the world: people in Japan bow, adults in France, Italy and Spain often greet with cheek kisses, and in New Zealand people say "Kia ora" and may share a hongi, pressing noses and foreheads.
explain: A boy from Chicago in Dublin is asked "What's the craic?"; he learns it means "How are things?" and answers "Not much, and you?"
fact: A good handshake is firm but not hard, for about two or three shakes.
fact: If your hand is dirty, wet or sticky, you say sorry and wave or smile instead.
fact: If you have a cold, you explain and wave instead of shaking hands, because germs spread through hands.
fact: After a match the teams shake hands and say "Good game", whether they won or lost.
fact: Some people do not shake hands, and you must respect that without insisting.
fact: A wave greets someone when you are too far away to talk.
fact: A wave from a stranger may be meant for someone behind you, and you never go over to a stranger.
fact: In English-speaking countries looking at a person's face while you talk shows you are listening and being honest.
fact: Many people in Japan bow to greet and to thank, and a small bow back is polite.
fact: In France, Italy and Spain adults often greet with cheek kisses, but a child may follow their lead or simply wave or shake hands.
fact: "Kia ora" is a Maori greeting used in New Zealand that means hello and good health.
fact: "How's it going?" from someone walking past is a greeting, and "Good, thanks!" is the expected answer.
exercise: What makes a good handshake? | Firm but not hard, for two or three shakes | As hard as you can, so they feel your strength | Loose and floppy, so it does not hurt | Very long, for at least ten shakes
exercise: Your hands are sticky from lunch when someone wants to shake. What do you do? | Say "Sorry, my hands are messy" and wave or smile | Shake anyway and say nothing | Wipe your hands on their sleeve | Hide your hands behind your back and walk away without a word
exercise: You have a cold and someone offers a handshake. What is kind? | Explain you have a cold and wave instead | Shake hands, then cough into your other hand | Refuse without a word | Shake hands but only for a moment
exercise: Your team lost the match. What do you do at the end? | Shake hands with the other team and say "Good game" | Walk off without shaking hands | Shake hands only with the players you like and ignore the others | Tell them they only won by luck
exercise: A woman says she does not shake hands for religious reasons. What do you do? | Do not insist, and smile, nod or put a hand on your heart | Keep holding out your hand until she gives in and shakes it | Tell her it is rude not to shake | Ask her to explain her religion to you
exercise: You see a friend far away across the playground. How do you say hello? | Wave to them | Shout until the whole playground hears | Throw something to get their attention | Wait until break is over
exercise: A person you do not know waves at you. What is sensible? | It may be for someone behind you; smile if you like, but do not go over | Run straight over to them at once to find out what they want and who they are | Wave back and walk to them | Shout to ask who they are
exercise: Your family drives away after dropping you off at camp. What is a friendly goodbye? | Wave goodbye to them | Turn your back without a word | Shake hands with the car | Bow deeply until they are gone
exercise: You keep looking at the floor while you talk. What is better in English-speaking countries? | Look at the person's face from time to time | Stare into their eyes without blinking | Close your eyes to concentrate | Look at your phone to seem relaxed
exercise: You visit a Japanese family and they bow to you. What is polite? | A small bow back | A loud high five | Ignore it, since bowing is only for adults | Laugh because it looks unusual
exercise: In Italy an adult greets you with cheek kisses. What can a child do? | Follow the adult's lead, or politely wave or shake hands | Push them away because it is wrong | Run off and hide behind a parent | Kiss everybody in the room on both cheeks, even people you do not know
exercise: A New Zealander says "Kia ora". What does it mean? | Hello and good health, in Maori | Goodbye and good luck, in French | Thank you very much, in Spanish | Excuse me, in Irish
exercise: What is a hongi? | A traditional Maori greeting of pressing noses and foreheads | A kind of deep Japanese bow from the waist, used for saying sorry | A special Irish handshake | An Australian way to wave goodbye
exercise: Someone in Ireland asks "What's the craic?" What do they mean? | "How are things?" or "What is the news?" | "Where is the bathroom?" or "Which way is the toilet?" | "Did you break something?" | "What time is it?"
exercise: A Canadian walking past says "How's it going?" What is the expected answer? | A quick "Good, thanks!" | A long story about your whole week | Nothing, because it is a rude question | "Why do you want to know?"
test: Why is it kinder to wave than to shake hands when you have a cold? | Germs spread through hands, and waving protects the other person | Because a cold makes your hands too weak to shake | Because shaking hands is not allowed in winter | Because a wave is always more polite than a handshake, cold or not
test: A girl in Melbourne gives a handshake so hard that her uncle winces. What should she change? | Grip firmly but not hard | Make her hand completely floppy | Shake with both hands at once | Stop shaking hands for ever
test: A boy in Texas loses the final and feels angry. What shows good sportsmanship? | Shaking hands and saying "Good game" anyway | Refusing to shake hands until he calms down | Telling the winners that the referee cheated | Shaking hands but looking away angrily
test: A visitor in your class does not shake hands and puts a hand on his heart instead. How do you answer? | Smile or nod back, respecting his custom | Grab his hand to shake it anyway | Ask the teacher to make him shake hands | Laugh and copy him to make a joke
test: Why might a stranger's wave not be for you? | It is often meant for someone behind you | Because strangers never wave | Because waving is only for goodbyes | Because only children wave at each other
test: Your family visits Japan and a shopkeeper bows as you leave. Which reply fits? | A small bow back | A hongi | Cheek kisses | A secret handshake
test: An American walking past your classroom says "How's it going?" Why is a short answer right? | It is a greeting, not a real question | Because Americans do not like long answers about anything | Because you must never talk to Americans | Because long answers are only for the phone
test: Why is it good to learn greetings like "Kia ora" or "What's the craic?" | Knowing local customs helps you understand and respect people | Because you must use them in every country | Because the English words we use at home are not polite enough there | Because they are secret passwords
test: got a cold, coach wants us all to shake hands | Explain and wave instead | Shake hands anyway | Cough into your hand and shake | Hide in the changing room
test: we lost 4-0, do we still shake hands | Yes, shake hands and say good game | No, only winners shake hands | No, walk off to show you're upset | Only shake the referee's hand
test: in NZ someone says kia ora to me, what's that | A Maori greeting meaning hello and good health | A way of saying goodbye forever when someone is leaving the country | A question about the weather | A word for thank you only
test: random lady waved at me across the street, go over? | No, it may be meant for someone else; never go to a stranger | Yes, run over and say hi | Yes, if she waves twice | Cross the road and ask her what she wants, it would be rude not to
explain: A boy in Ireland squeezed new classmates' hands as hard as he could to look strong; one girl's hand hurt and she told the teacher, who asked him to stop.
explain: A girl from Leeds on holiday in Japan walked into a home with her shoes on; the family was upset, and she felt embarrassed when her mum explained the custom.
fact: A good handshake is firm but gentle; squeezing hard can hurt.
fact: Customs differ between countries, and learning them before you visit avoids upsetting people.
fact: In Japan, and in many homes elsewhere, people take their shoes off at the door, and guests do the same.
test: gonna crush his hand when we shake, show who's boss | Shake firmly but gently, crushing hurts | Squeeze as hard as you can | Shake for a full minute so he knows you mean it | Pull him towards you hard
test: visiting a family that takes shoes off inside, i keep mine on | Take them off, it's their custom and shows respect | Keep them on, it's your style and they're your shoes | Only take one shoe off | Wipe them and walk in
test: why learn other ppl's customs before a trip | So you don't upset people by mistake | So you can laugh at them | It doesn't matter abroad | Only the adults on the trip need to know any of that stuff
test: did u hear Finn crushed a girl's hand so hard shaking it "her fingers snapped". what's probs exaggerated | Snapped fingers; but squeezing hard really can hurt | That a handshake should be firm but gentle and never squeeze hard | That her hand hurt after it | That the teacher asked him to stop
test: my mate says a girl went in a house in Japan with shoes on n "got kicked out the country". real lesson? | Learn local customs before you visit, to avoid upset | Japan bans all tourists who wear shoes | Customs do not matter when you are on holiday | Shoes only matter if they are muddy
test: kids say the new boy won't shake hands cos he's "weird". pass it on? | No, some people do not shake hands, respect it | Yes, everyone should know he is rude | Yes, and make him shake hands next time | Yes, but only tell it as a joke
exam: State the qualities of a correctly executed handshake. | Firm but not hard, for two or three shakes | Very hard, for as long as can be held | Limp and loose, for a single brief shake | Very gentle, with the fingertips only
exam: It is given that a person has a cold. Determine the appropriate alternative to shaking hands and the reason for it. | Explain and wave, as germs spread through hands | Shake hands faster, as germs need time to pass | Shake with the left hand, as it holds no germs | Bow very deeply, as the custom is required
exam: Ascertain the meaning of the Maori greeting "Kia ora" as used in New Zealand. | Hello and good health | Goodbye and safe travels | Thank you and good night | Welcome to the meeting house
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the expected reply to "How's it going?" from a passer-by? | "Good, thanks!", as it is a greeting | A full account of one's day so far | Silence, as it needs no reply at all | A question as to why they ask
